4.Majority rule. As of this printing, thirty-three courts, with fair consistency, condition the existence of a duty on plaintiff-foreseeability. Only four jurisdictions clearly follow Judge Andrews in holding that plaintiff-foreseeability is solely a matter for proximate cause. Three of the four states reserving plaintiff-foreseeability to proximate cause have done so in recent years, each by express adoption of the recently published Restatement (Third) of Torts, which adopts the Andrewsian approach. See Restatement (Third) of Torts: Liability for Physical and Emotional Harm § 29, reporters’ note to cmt. n. It remains to be seen whether the Restatement’s position takes hold as a general trend. In the remaining fourteen jurisdictions, the proper doctrinal home for plaintiff-foreseeability is unclear. Some jurisdictions are hard to discern because courts conceptualize Palsgraf-like scenarios in terms of harm-foreseeability rather than plaintiff-foreseeability. In other jurisdictions, courts analyze plaintiff-foreseeability only in certain types of cases—particularly those involving purely economic harm or third-party crime—but do not seem to have embraced it as a general principle. Finally, in some jurisdictions, the case law is simply contradictory or insufficiently specific. Three such jurisdictions appear to lean toward considering plaintiff-foreseeability in duty. Five lean toward proximate cause. The research underlying this Note may be found in W. Jonathan Cardi, The Hidden Legacy of Palsgraf: Modern Duty Law in Microcosm, 91 B.U. L. Rev. 1873 (2011).

5.Some commentators have questioned how frequently Palsgraf-type scenarios arise in the case law. They are more common than one might expect. Consider one modern-day analog, the case of Mussivand v. David, 45 Ohio St.3d 314, 544 N.E.2d 265 (1989). In that case, the plaintiff sued his wife’s adulterous lover for having passed a sexually-transmitted disease to his wife, which she then passed to the plaintiff. The court readily found that a general duty exists on the part of someone with an STD to use reasonable care when engaging in sex—which might include abstaining from sex altogether, or at least warning one’s partner. The court, however, saw the issue to be whether the defendant owed such a duty to his partner’s husband. In holding for the plaintiff, the court offered the following reasoning:

In this case appellant, allegedly infected with a venereal disease, engaged in sexual relations with a married woman. A reasonably prudent person would anticipate that a wife and husband will engage in sexual relations. In addition, [defendant] is a medical doctor who, more than most people, should be aware of the method of transmitting a venereal disease, its likelihood of spreading through sexual contact, and its potentially devastating effect. If one negligently exposes a married person to a sexually transmissible disease without informing that person of his exposure, it is reasonable to anticipate that the disease may be transmitted to the
347
married person’s spouse. Hence liability to a third party for failure to disclose to the original sexual partner turns on whether, under all the circumstances, injury to the third-party spouse was foreseeable.

6.Scope of the inquiry. In jurisdictions where plaintiff-foreseeability is decided as part of duty, courts face an issue similar to that relevant to risk-foreseeability—how to define the subject of the analysis. In an attempt to render plaintiff-foreseeability decisions categorical, a minority of jurisdictions claim to examine foreseeability of the “class of plaintiffs of which the plaintiff is a member.” Thus, for example, the Montana Supreme Court held that the duty of police in a high-speed chase was not to foresee harm to the particular plaintiff but to “people using the streets and highways where the chase occurred.” Eklund v. Trost, 335 Mont. 112, 151 P.3d 870, 880 (2006). On one level, this appears to be properly categorical duty reasoning—the court put itself in the shoes of the police defendants at the time of the chase and imagined the class of people potentially injured by the defendants’ actions, limiting the scope of the defendants’ duty to that class of people. But the court’s analysis did not stop there. It also applied the second part of the standard—determining whether the plaintiff was a member of that class. This second step requires a narrow, fact-specific judgment. The reality is that even where courts express the plaintiff-foreseeability standard in categorical terms, the standard is commonly little more than window-dressing for a fact-specific examination of plaintiff-foreseeability.

The inherent instability of categorical formulations of plaintiff-foreseeability stems in part from the lack of any principle by which courts define the scope of the foreseeable class. For example, although the court in Eklund defined the class quite broadly—“people using the streets and highways where the chase occurred”—the class would have excluded a plaintiff injured by the careening police chase while watching it from the front porch of his house. A broader definition—for example, “people present in the area of the chase”—would allow recovery by such a victim. In the alternative, a court might deny liability to a broader class of cases by defining the class more narrowly—for instance, “people asleep in their car on the side of the road,” “people interfering with the chase,” or “drunk drivers.” There appear to be no guidelines according to which courts determine how narrowly or broadly to define the class of plaintiffs into which a particular plaintiff must fit. In fact, courts rarely even disclose the reasoning by which they have defined the class.

7.Judge/jury question. The judge/jury issue is often cited as the most important consequence of Palsgraf. Andrews’s dissent would have upheld the trial jury’s plaintiff verdict on the grounds that what Andrews saw as the relevant issue—the peculiarity of the sequence of events that led to Mrs. Palsgraf’s injury—was properly decided by the jury as a matter of proximate cause. Judge Cardozo’s majority opinion instead overturned the jury’s verdict and dismissed the plaintiff’s claim on no-duty grounds. The common interpretation of Cardozo’s opinion is that because plaintiff-foreseeability is a duty question, and because duty is determined by the court, the jury had no
348
place in deciding plaintiff-foreseeability. But this explanation is belied by the text of Cardozo’s opinion, in which he stated:

The risk reasonably to be perceived defines the duty to be obeyed, and risk imports relation; it is risk to another or to others within the range of apprehension. . . . The range of reasonable apprehension is at times a question for the court, and at times, if varying inferences are possible, a question for the jury. (emphasis added)

This part of Cardozo’s opinion is jarring because it contradicts the basic axiom that duty’s existence is to be decided by the court, not the jury. As it turns out, although this portion of Cardozo’s opinion is rarely cited, an extraordinary number of courts have reached the same conclusion independently, leaving foreseeability (of risk or plaintiff) to the jury, even in the duty context. Specifically, of forty-seven jurisdictions that arguably assign foreseeability to duty, twenty hold or lean toward holding that foreseeability is to be decided by the jury, and sixteen hold or lean toward holding that foreseeability is for the court. In eleven jurisdictions, the question remains closely divided or unaddressed. In addition, recall that four jurisdictions explicitly purge duty of foreseeability altogether, thus leaving foreseeability to the jury as part of proximate cause. The research underlying this Note may be found in Cardi, The Hidden Legacy of Palsgraf, supra.

