issue was whether the actions of a nine-year-old boy who pushed against the pane sufficiently interrupted the causal chain to absolve the defendant of liability. Panics leading to deaths at crowded stadiums continue to occur. Indeed, in Alcock v. Chief Constable of the South Yorkshire Police, [1992] 1 A.C. 330 (1991), the House of Lords did not rule out the possibility that someone, who witnessed on live TV a close relative actually being caught up in a scrum of people, might have an action against the police for failing to have adequate crowd control measures in place. In that case, however, the plaintiffs could not recover because the BBC, following its usual practice when showing crowd scenes, had blurred out the faces of individuals pictured in the transmitted images.

2.AFFIRMATIVE DUTIES TO WARN, PROTECT OR RESCUE

a.The General Rule: No Duty to Warn, Protect or Rescue

Section B.1 of this chapter explored the foundational duty rule that if one’s conduct creates a risk of harm (or, depending on the jurisdiction, a foreseeable risk to a foreseeable plaintiff), one owes a duty to engage in that conduct with reasonable care. This rule focuses on one’s actions and the manner in which one must conduct those actions. In this next section, we explore duties with regard to one’s inaction. Specifically, we ask: What duty of care does one owe to warn, protect, or rescue another from a risk created by some other source? The foundational answer is simple: None. One generally owes no duty to warn, protect, or rescue another from a risk created by some other source.

To use a rather crass but canonical example, suppose that you are out for a Sunday stroll along a nearby railroad track, and you run across a swaddled baby lying on the tracks, seemingly abandoned. Although you might feel a strong moral duty to move the baby to safety, you have no legal duty to do so. The rule is captured by the Restatement Second as follows.

§ 314 of the Restatement (Second) of Torts

The fact that the actor realizes or should realize that action on his part is necessary for another’s aid or protection does not of itself impose upon him a duty to take such action.

